Qadam Flow for Debian
=====================

This packaging mirrors the official Docker image (ghcr.io/aiqadam/qadam-flow)
onto the Debian filesystem:

  Docker container      Debian package         systemd unit
  -----------------     --------------------   -------------------------
  app x1                qadam-flow             qadam-flow-master.service
  worker x5             qadam-flow-worker      qadam-flow-worker.service
  postgres             postgresql-18-pgvector  (system service)
  redis (valkey)        redis-server           (system service)

NOTE on apt installability: `nodejs (>= 24.21)` and `postgresql-18-pgvector`
are not in the plain Debian archive — the target hosts carry their Node.js
and pgvector repositories (Debian forky in the build environment ships both).
Without them apt refuses with an unsatisfied-dependency error naming one
missing package, which is this prerequisite and not a package bug.

  common payload shared by both:     qadam-flow-common
  (vendored node_modules under /usr/lib/qadam-flow/node_modules, the built
   packages under /usr/lib/qadam-flow/packages and /usr/lib/qadam-flow/dist,
   the web build under /usr/share/qadam-flow/web, the isolate sandbox
   configuration under /usr/local/etc/isolate)

A co-hosted install is simply:

  apt install qadam-flow qadam-flow-worker

The master postinst provisions everything automatically: it creates the
`qadam` system user, mints AP_ENCRYPTION_KEY / AP_JWT_SECRET / the database
password into /etc/qadam-flow/master.conf (0600) on first install — pre-set
values are never overwritten by upgrades — creates the qadam_flow PostgreSQL
role, database and pgvector extension, and the worker postinst mints
AP_WORKER_TOKEN the same way docker-entrypoint.sh does when the master's
config lives on the same host. Qadam Flow then listens on AP_PORT (3000); put
nginx in front for TLS and edit AP_FRONTEND_URL in /etc/qadam-flow/master.conf
to the public https:// URL.

Distributed workers (worker-only host)
--------------------------------------
The worker does NOT depend on PostgreSQL or Redis — it talks to the master
over HTTP only. On a second host:

  1. apt install qadam-flow-common qadam-flow-worker
  2. point /etc/qadam-flow/worker.conf at the master:
       AP_WORKER_API_URL=http://<master-host>:3000
     (or an https:// URL behind the reverse proxy)
  3. on the MASTER host run qadam-flow-mint-worker-token — it prints the
     derived token and never the master's AP_JWT_SECRET — then set the
     output as AP_WORKER_TOKEN in this host's /etc/qadam-flow/worker.conf;
     when both packages share a host, the worker postinst mints by itself
  4. systemctl enable --now qadam-flow-worker

Secrets stay on each host: only the master host needs the database and
AP_ENCRYPTION_KEY; workers are stateless and carry no run history.

Sandboxed execution (optional)
------------------------------
AP_EXECUTION_MODE in /etc/qadam-flow/worker.conf defaults to UNSANDBOXED, like
the default Docker compose file. The isolate-based modes need:

  * the /usr/src/node_modules bridge — shipped by qadam-flow-common as a
    symlink to /usr/lib/qadam-flow/node_modules, because the isolate
    wrapper hardcodes that path for vendoring the sandboxed engine
  * extra privileges on the worker unit, same trade-off as
    docker-compose.sandboxed.yml:

      systemctl edit qadam-flow-worker
        [Service]
        AmbientCapabilities=CAP_SYS_ADMIN
        AppArmorProfile=
        SystemCallFilter=

  (and for AP_NETWORK_MODE=STRICT also: AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_ADMIN)

See https://flow.aiqadam.org/docs (sandboxing) for why CAP_SYS_ADMIN is not
granted by default.

Conffiles, upgrades and purge
-----------------------------
/etc/qadam-flow/{master,worker}.conf are conffiles with 0600 permissions:
edits survive upgrades, and dpkg asks before overwriting. Only
CHANGE_ME-looking placeholders are ever rewritten. `apt purge qadam-flow`
removes /var/lib/qadam-flow (qadam version store), /var/cache/qadam-flow and
the `qadam` user (if no qadam-flow-worker package remains); the PostgreSQL
role/database are deliberately kept and the postrm prints the DROP
statements.

